From c3af9d8372ee9364696d017f2e14367375a9fda9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_sched_tc_fixed <>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 19:27:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] apps/examples/testcase: add priority and affinity scenario
 families

Phase 3 of the scenario test rollout.  Priority and affinity are the two
pieces of core scheduling semantics with no behavioural coverage at all
today: the existing tests confirm that a priority or a CPU mask can be
stored and read back, which says nothing about whether the scheduler acts
on either.

The harness gains what these families need.  A workload may now carry per
worker priorities and per worker affinity masks rather than one value shared
by all, and workers may be asked to call sched_getcpu on every iteration and
record both the set of CPUs they were observed on and any sample taken
outside their mask.  The workload lifecycle is split into start, sample and
finish so that a scenario can act while the load is running, which is what
lets a task's priority or affinity be changed mid flight and the effect
measured in a second window; stc_run_workload is now those three in order.
Because the workload structure gained optional members, stc_workload_init
is introduced and the existing scenarios are converted to it, so that a
partially initialised structure can never take its per worker arrays from
whatever was on the stack.

stc_sched_priority adds four scenarios.  PRI-01 saturates every CPU with low
priority work and measures how long a released high priority task takes to
run, where the failure mode is waiting for the load to finish seconds later.
PRI-02 runs a ladder of distinct priorities with more tasks than CPUs and
asserts absolutely, with no tolerance, that exactly the top ncpus tasks
accumulate work and every task below them records zero.  PRI-03 promotes a
task that is ready to run but assigned to no CPU, and PRI-04 demotes a task
that is currently running; these reach different code paths and are tested
separately for that reason.

PRI-04 takes its minimum over the peers only, excluding the demoted worker.
After the demotion that worker is the least important runnable task, so on
an oversubscribed machine it is the one that should now be starved, and
including it would assert the opposite of the property under test.  A second
assertion checks that the demoted worker actually gave up ground, so the
peers cannot appear to pass for some unrelated reason.

stc_sched_affinity adds five.  AFF-01 pins two tasks to one CPU so they
preempt each other constantly, and asserts not one sample outside the mask
across at least a thousand samples.  AFF-02 narrows the mask of an already
running task to exclude the CPU it is on, which forces a migration rather
than a placement, and allows a settling period before judging placement
because sched_setaffinity returning does not mean the task has moved yet.
AFF-03 deliberately unbalances the run queues and checks that queue depth on
one CPU neither starves the other nor causes pinned tasks to be migrated for
balance.  AFF-04 checks that unpinned tasks spread one per CPU, which per
task counters alone cannot detect because crowding every task onto one CPU
still produces healthy looking counts.  AFF-05 locates an IDLE task by
scanning for priority zero, rather than assuming a PID, and confirms its
affinity cannot be changed; it asserts the negative errno that
sched_setaffinity actually returns rather than the ERROR and errno pair
POSIX specifies, matching what the existing API level test asserts on its
non-SMP path.

The affinity family compiles away entirely without CONFIG_SMP and reports a
skip, as does AFF on a single CPU configuration.  Both the SMP and non-SMP
compilation paths are warning free under -Wall -Wextra.
